WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:01.589
Next question today.

00:00:01.589 --> 00:00:05.750
Hey David, should teachers be allowed to discuss
politics in the classroom?

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:07.060
Why or why not?

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:11.460
Um, so first of all, we've got to understand
that there's a kind of practical element to

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.410
the question of politics in the classroom.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:15.940
And then there's also a question of taboo.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:20.960
So when we talk about whether your dentists
should discuss politics, it's a different

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.880
conversation than whether teachers should
discuss politics.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:28.730
And discuss is also different from advocate
for or lobby, right?

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:33.590
When your dentists discuss his politics, it's
really just a risk to them because if you

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:37.860
don't agree with their politics or you don't
like the fact that they're talking about politics,

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:41.830
you can just go to another dentist assuming
that there's a sort of market of dentists

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:45.070
that are reasonably priced and accessible
in your area.

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:51.830
Um, when teachers talk about politics, it's
different because of the idea in society that

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:56.760
teachers are in a position of authority when
it comes to knowledge.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:04.280
And we presumably, I think most parents don't
want teachers biasing our children, particularly

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:09.799
if they're biasing our children in ways that
are not in line with our personal politics

00:01:09.799 --> 00:01:14.859
because the children may not be able to differentiate
from just like normal classroom material and

00:01:14.859 --> 00:01:16.409
the teacher advocating politics.

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:19.819
This is why advertising to children is actually
so pernicious.

00:01:19.819 --> 00:01:24.500
There's a period of time when children don't
understand the difference between just fun

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:28.840
programming and something that's trying to
get you to buy a product.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:30.829
And that's why advertising kids is pernicious.

00:01:30.829 --> 00:01:34.829
And presumably the same thing could happen
when teachers mix classroom material with

00:01:34.829 --> 00:01:35.829
politics.

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:39.840
I do think that if we're going to talk about
the seriously, it's gotta be divided between

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:45.590
sort of like elementary school, Middle School,
high school starts to become a gray area and

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:46.590
college.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:51.109
I truly couldn't tell you the politics of
almost any of my elementary school teachers.

00:01:51.109 --> 00:01:55.500
And I do think that sticking to the curriculum
in a way that's not overtly political does

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.520
make sense in American public schools.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:03.520
But in a lot of classes we just don't need
politics in math class for example, there

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.860
are areas though, once you get into science,
once you get into human geography, there are

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:14.090
political realities that are either implicit
or unavoidable in those areas.

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:20.730
And I still think in those cases teachers
should be able to keep the advocacy to a minimum.

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:24.910
Don't advocate necessarily for particular
candidates, for example.

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:28.540
But in science class you've got to talk about
the environment.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.650
How do you not talk about climate change?

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:33.980
Uh, I don't believe climate change is political.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:35.000
It's just the reality.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:39.200
But there are people who do, but teachers
have to be able to talk about that.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:42.670
It's just the world around us that they're
describing in college.

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:47.930
It gets different, I think, but more call,
more, more college politics in the classroom

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:52.840
is okay, uh, advocating for or against specific
candidates.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:55.980
Maybe it should be kept out of the classroom,
but we're talking about adults.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:59.591
We're talking about people who are better
able to identify what's a personal view, what

00:02:59.591 --> 00:03:00.760
is coursework?

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:05.819
Um, there's no question that the underlying
philosophy and subject matter and a lot of

00:03:05.819 --> 00:03:10.299
college classes is inherently political and
pretending that it can just be kept out is

00:03:10.299 --> 00:03:11.299
not reasonable.

00:03:11.299 --> 00:03:15.959
And you're actually starting to do damage
to students if you artificially keep politics

00:03:15.959 --> 00:03:16.959
out.

00:03:16.959 --> 00:03:20.099
So as with most things, the answer is it depends.

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:21.390
It depends what we're talking about.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:26.200
It depends what age we're talking about, but
some political realities in the classroom

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:30.860
or not only unavoidable, they should be, uh,
uh, advisable.

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:35.900
They're actually advisable so that people,
students have a realistic understanding of

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:36.459
what is going on in the world.

